# QMT Agent Configuration System

This document describes the durable design and operating rules of the QMT Agent configuration system.
It documents configuration semantics, not live configuration values.
Use `get_config` when the current effective value matters.
Never store secret values in this file.

## Configuration Layers

Effective application configuration is built from these layers, from lowest to highest priority:

1. Built-in defaults in `qmt_agent.config.DEFAULT_CONFIG` for legacy application settings
2. Repository configuration at `config/qmt.toml`
3. Local instance configuration at `<root>/qmt.toml`
4. Runtime-only changes to the current `AppConfig`

The runtime policy values listed below are TOML-only; they have no Python fallback.

A persistent runtime update is written to `<root>/qmt.toml` and therefore becomes part of the local instance configuration for later processes.

## Configuration Lifecycle

| lifecycle | keys | behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| immutable | `paths.root`, `bootstrap.*` | Cannot be changed through runtime configuration. |
| restart-required | `paths.data`, `paths.workspace`, `paths.state`, `model.name`, `model.base_url`, `mcp.default_timeout_seconds`, `execution.durable_job_dir` | `persist=false` is rejected. `persist=true` writes the next-start value but does not change the current runtime; restart QMT Agent to apply it. |
| hot | runtime policy and observability settings not listed above | Validated and applied to the current `AppConfig`; persistence is optional. |

`get_config` reports the value currently used by this process. A persisted restart-required update therefore continues to report the old value until the application is restarted.

## Root Directory

The repository configuration defines `paths.root`.
`paths.root` is bootstrap configuration.
The local `<root>/qmt.toml` must not redefine it.
The root contains local QMT Agent state including:

- `qmt.toml`
- `mcp.toml`
- the configured data directory
- the configured workspace directory
- the configured state directory

These files are outside the Git repository.

The generic `paths.data` boundary is initialized separately from the Agent workspace. The current CNEquity transition backend is an implementation detail behind `qmt_agent.data`; its `<root>/cnequity.toml` configuration and default `<data>/cnequity` lake subdirectory are not part of the QMT Agent-facing configuration or tool contract. Existing backend configuration and its internal paths remain owned by CNEquity.

## Root-relative Paths

`paths.data`, `paths.workspace`, and `paths.state` are relative to `paths.root`.
They must not be absolute and must not escape the root.
All three must be strict subdirectories of the root and must not equal or contain one another after path resolution.
The SQLite session database is stored under the state directory.
Long-term Agent memory does not use a database.
Long-term memory is maintained as Markdown in the workspace, with `MEMORY.md` as the entry point.

## Project Configuration

Repository defaults live in:
`config/qmt.toml`
Project configuration may be committed to Git and must not contain secrets.

The runtime policy sections below are TOML-only and must be present in the effective configuration:

- `[runtime].default_timezone`
- `[execution].default_timeout_seconds`, `max_timeout_seconds`, `background_job_dir`, `background_job_id_chars`, `background_log_chunk_bytes`, `background_log_max_bytes`, `background_log_retained_bytes`, `background_output_tail_chars`, `background_status_timeout_seconds`, `background_stop_timeout_seconds`, and `durable_job_dir`
- `[explore].max_full_read_bytes`, `max_read_chars`, `max_search_results`, and `max_search_snippet_chars`
- `[calculate].max_expression_chars`, `max_nodes`, `max_integer_bits`, and `max_abs_exponent`
- `[observability].summary_threshold`
- `[mcp].default_timeout_seconds`

They are validated at startup and again before an update is persisted. `background_job_dir` must remain relative to the configured workspace, `durable_job_dir` must remain a strict subdirectory of the configured state directory, background job IDs cannot exceed the UUID hex width, retained log bytes and log chunk bytes cannot exceed maximum log bytes, and returned output characters cannot exceed retained log bytes.

## Managed Background Jobs

- Observe and confirm a managed job before stopping it; stop only after an explicit user request and the approved `stop_background_job` tool call.
- Pass the complete receipt `job_id` returned by `exec_command(background=true)` or a managed lifecycle run; displayed PID and PGID values are for observation only and must never be signaled directly.
- `stop_background_job` terminates the entire managed process group with SIGTERM, waits `execution.background_stop_timeout_seconds`, and escalates to SIGKILL when needed; never terminate only the worker PID.
- A stop receipt reports the `job_id`, terminal `status`, process-group termination, and signal-escalation outcome; jobs with an exclusive lock also report `lock_released` before they may be replaced by another exclusive job.
- `stopped` or `cancelled` is not successful completion; `unknown`, `lost`, and `stop_failed` must not be guessed into success, completion, or lock release.
- Report a stop only after its receipt reaches a terminal status and inspect its bounded output or status as needed; do not infer completion from a signal or a disappearing PID.

## Local Configuration

Machine- and user-specific overrides live in:
`<root>/qmt.toml`
Secrets live in its `[secrets]` table.
Secret values must never be copied into workspace memory.

## Reading Configuration

Use `get_config` to inspect effective application configuration.
Secret values returned to the Agent are redacted.
Prefer `get_config` over memory whenever the current effective value matters.

## Modifying Configuration

Use `update_config` for Agent-managed changes to normal application configuration.
Configuration modification requires user approval.

Rules:

- `paths.root` and `bootstrap.*` cannot be changed at runtime.
- Restart-required keys must use `persist=true`; the returned result reports `requires_restart=true` and `applied=false`.
- Hot keys report `applied=true` when the current runtime is updated.
- Unknown normal configuration keys cannot be created.
- Values must retain their expected type. Strings, booleans, integers, and floating-point values are supported.
- Root-relative paths are validated before being applied.
- Runtime policy values must satisfy their type, range, and cross-field constraints before being applied.
- `persist=true` writes the override to `<root>/qmt.toml`.
- `persist=false` only changes the current `AppConfig`.

The Agent-facing config tool does not modify secrets.
Secrets must currently be edited outside that tool.

## MCP Configuration

MCP server configuration is local instance state and is stored separately at:
`<root>/mcp.toml`
It is not tracked by Git.
The user-managed `mcp.toml` and configuration tools support only `streamable_http`. The built-in read-only market-data query server uses an internal stdio connection and is not configured in `mcp.toml` or exposed through the Agent configuration tools.
Use:

- `list_mcp_servers` to inspect configured servers
- `configure_mcp_server` to add or update a server
- `remove_mcp_server` to remove a server

MCP configuration changes require user approval.

## MCP Secrets

Real secret values should not be written directly into `mcp.toml`.
Reference configured secrets with placeholders such as:
`${TAVILY_API_KEY}`
The real value belongs in the `[secrets]` table of `<root>/qmt.toml`.
MCP loading expands these placeholders from AppConfig secrets.
The application-level `mcp.default_timeout_seconds` is used only when a server does not define its own `timeout`. Changes to this default affect MCP connections after QMT Agent is restarted.

## Initialization

Persistent local runtime state is initialized separately from configuration loading.
Project-provided bootstrap workspace files are copied into the user workspace during its initial bootstrap.
Bootstrap files never overwrite existing user-owned workspace files.
After bootstrap, workspace memory belongs to the user and the Agent. Project bootstrap files are not automatically synced over it.

## Design Principles

Keep the configuration system explicit and small.

Prefer:

- repository defaults
- local overrides
- runtime mutation
- explicit Agent tools
- user approval for side effects

Do not introduce Provider, Registry, Adapter, or Manager layers unless a concrete requirement makes them necessary.
